Reasoning: Red means an unassigned primary. Which shards are down, and did a node drop?
<|call:es_cluster_health|> {"level": "indices", "index": "orders-2026.07"}
System Response: status red; data nodes 8 of expected 9; unassigned_shards 4; orders-2026.07 red, active_shards 8.
Recovery reasoning: A node is out. Map the copies.
<|call:es_cat_shards|> {"index": "orders-2026.07", "h": "shard,prirep,state,node"}
System Response: 0 p UNASSIGNED; 0 r UNASSIGNED; 3 p UNASSIGNED; 3 r UNASSIGNED; shards 1,2,4,5 STARTED across es-data-1..6.
Recovery reasoning: Shards 0 and 3 lost both copies. Ask why the primary cannot be placed.
<|call:es_allocation_explain|> {"index": "orders-2026.07", "shard": 0, "primary": true}
System Response: can_allocate: no_valid_shard_copy — a previous copy of the primary existed but a valid copy could not be found on the nodes in the cluster.
Recovery reasoning: Read literally that means data loss and invites allocate_empty_primary. But explain surveys only nodes currently in the cluster, and one is missing: replicas of 0 and 3 were already unassigned since last week's watermark breach, so es-data-7 held the sole copies when it vanished at 04:12. Recover it, never reroute empty.
<|call:ssh_exec|> {"host": "es-data-7", "cmd": "dmesg | tail -3 && sudo systemctl restart elasticsearch"}
System Response: [04:11:58] XFS (nvme1n1): task hung 122s on /var/lib/elasticsearch; elasticsearch restarted; node rejoined.
Recovery reasoning: Node is back with its shards. Verify recovery.
<|call:es_cluster_health|> {"index": "orders-2026.07", "wait_for_status": "green", "timeout": "5m"}
System Response: status green; data nodes 9; unassigned_shards 0; active_shards_percent 100.0.
Resolution: Writes restored, zero loss, 380M docs intact. Root cause: es-data-7 hung on a stalled XFS mount while holding the only copies of shards 0 and 3 (their replicas lost earlier to a watermark breach); no_valid_shard_copy meant no copy among survivors, not lost data.

Reasoning: FORBIDDEN/12 is the flood-stage watermark block — Elasticsearch sets index.blocks.read_only_allow_delete when a disk crosses 95%. Confirm the block is on the index.
<|call:es_get_settings|> {"index": "logs-app-2026.07.03", "filter_path": "*.settings.index.blocks*"}
System Response: logs-app-2026.07.03: index.blocks.read_only_allow_delete: "true"
Recovery reasoning: Block confirmed. Check per-node disk against the watermarks.
<|call:es_cat_allocation|> {"h": "node,disk.percent,disk.used,disk.total"}
System Response: es-data-1 58% 4.6tb/8tb; es-data-2 61% 4.9tb/8tb; es-data-3 63% 5.0tb/8tb.
Recovery reasoning: Every node is far below the 90% high watermark, which reads as "the disk problem is over, writes should flow" — yet they do not, and that mismatch is the tell: automatic release of the flood-stage block only exists from 7.4 onward. On 7.2.1 the block is sticky: set at 06:02 when es-data-3 spiked to 96%, it survives any amount of freed space until removed by hand. Clear it explicitly.
<|call:es_put_settings|> {"index": "logs-app-*", "body": {"index.blocks.read_only_allow_delete": null}}
System Response: {"acknowledged": true} — setting removed on 9 indices matching logs-app-*.
Recovery reasoning: Prove writes work with a real document, not just a settings read.
<|call:es_index_doc|> {"index": "logs-app-2026.07.03", "doc": {"msg": "synthetic write probe", "ts": "2026-07-03T06:41:00Z"}}
System Response: {"result": "created", "_shards": {"total": 2, "successful": 2, "failed": 0}, "status": 201}
Resolution: Bulk error rate fell to zero within a minute. Root cause: es-data-3 crossed the 95% flood-stage watermark at 06:02 and Elasticsearch applied read_only_allow_delete; on 7.2.1 the block never auto-releases (that shipped in 7.4), so deleting 400GB fixed the graphs but not the block. Upgrade to 7.17 ticketed so future breaches self-heal.

Reasoning: The parent breaker at 95% of a 31GB heap is 29.4GB; trips mean real-memory demand briefly exceeds it. Check heap pressure across the data nodes.
<|call:es_cat_nodes|> {"h": "name,heap.percent,cpu,load_1m", "v": true}
System Response: es-d1 heap 58% cpu 41; es-d2 heap 61% cpu 44; es-d3 heap 57% cpu 38.
Recovery reasoning: Heap near 60% looks like no memory problem exists — but cat/nodes samples heap at one instant, usually right after a GC, while the parent breaker fires on real-memory spikes living for milliseconds inside request execution. Cumulative breaker counters tell the truth, not a snapshot.
<|call:es_nodes_stats|> {"metric": "breaker", "nodes": "es-d1,es-d2,es-d3"}
System Response: es-d2 parent: limit 29.4gb, estimated 28.9gb, tripped 3412 (3160 ten minutes ago); fielddata and request tripped 0; other nodes 0.
Recovery reasoning: 252 trips in ten minutes, all on es-d2 — something runs oversized aggregations there. Find the live search tasks.
<|call:es_tasks_list|> {"actions": "*search*", "detailed": true}
System Response: task Xb12:9871 on es-d2, running 11.4m: indices[events-*] terms agg on user_id size=250000 with top_hits sub-agg; source: kibana dashboard daily-actives-v2.
Recovery reasoning: 250k buckets of a high-cardinality keyword plus top_hits builds huge transient structures — exactly what fires the parent breaker while ingest stays healthy. Cancel it; the panel gets rebuilt on a paginated composite agg.
<|call:es_tasks_cancel|> {"task_id": "Xb12:9871", "reason": "oversized terms agg tripping parent breaker"}
System Response: {"acknowledged": true, "task": "Xb12:9871 cancelled"}
Resolution: Tripped counter flat for 30 minutes after cancellation (3412 -> 3412), dashboard 429s stopped. Root cause: the daily-actives-v2 panel ran a terms agg with size 250000 on user_id; the breaker rejected requests by design instead of letting the node OOM. Panel rewritten as a composite agg with size 1000 and after_key paging.

Reasoning: Bulk rejections mean a write thread pool queue overflowed. Check write pools across the data nodes.
<|call:es_cat_thread_pool|> {"thread_pool_patterns": "write", "h": "node_name,active,queue,rejected", "v": true}
System Response: es-d1 write active 8 queue 198 rejected 1842003; es-d2..d6 active 0-1 queue 0 rejected ~200.
Recovery reasoning: All rejections land on es-d1 while five nodes idle. Inspect the node.
<|call:es_cat_nodes|> {"h": "name,cpu,load_1m,heap.percent"}
System Response: es-d1 cpu 91 load 14.2 heap 74; es-d2..d6 cpu 12-16 load 1.1-1.8 heap 40-48.
Recovery reasoning: One node at 91% CPU beside idle peers reads as failing hardware and invites replacing the box — but a sick machine would also show it in heap, GC or disk, and those are normal. Skewed CPU means skewed work: check where the hot index's shards live before blaming metal.
<|call:es_cat_shards|> {"index": "events-ingest", "h": "shard,prirep,state,node"}
System Response: 0 p STARTED es-d1; 0 r STARTED es-d4 — exactly 1 primary, 1 replica.
Recovery reasoning: Created at cutover with the 7.x default single primary, so every bulk funnels through es-d1's 200-slot write queue. Shard count is immutable on a live index — roll the write alias to a 6-primary index.
<|call:es_create_index|> {"index": "events-ingest-000002", "body": {"settings": {"number_of_shards": 6, "number_of_replicas": 1}, "aliases": {"events-ingest-write": {"is_write_index": true}}}}
System Response: acknowledged true; events-ingest-write now targets events-ingest-000002; old index kept for reads.
Resolution: Rejected counter flat at 1842003 over the next 30 minutes, bulk failures 12% -> 0%, es-d1 cpu 91% -> 18%, write queues 0-6 across all six nodes. Root cause: the migration created events-ingest with the 7.x default of one primary shard, funnelling all writes through a single node's bounded write queue; the 6-primary write index spreads bulks cluster-wide.

Reasoning: Creeping old-gen with a flat workload means a resident consumer. Fielddata and caches are itemized in node stats — measure the suspects first.
<|call:es_nodes_stats|> {"metric": "indices,jvm", "filter_path": "nodes.*.indices,nodes.*.jvm.mem"}
System Response: per node: fielddata 41mb; query_cache 310mb; segments memory 3.1gb; heap used 26.4gb of 30gb (old gen 88%).
Recovery reasoning: 41MB kills the fielddata theory — but the itemized consumers total ~3.5GB while 26.4GB is resident. The unaccounted ~23GB lives where stats never itemize: cluster state and fixed per-shard overhead. With 40s state updates, that points at oversharding. Count shards.
<|call:es_cat_shards|> {"h": "index,state", "format": "json"}
System Response: 14208 shards across 1184 daily indices (logs-2023.05.01 through logs-2026.07.03, each 6 primaries x 1 replica), ~4736 per node; 1020 indices predate the 90-day retention line of 2026-04-04.
Recovery reasoning: 4736 shards per 30GB node is ~8x the 20-per-GB guideline (600). Retention is declared but unenforced. Deleting the 1020 out-of-retention indices removes 1020 x 12 = 12240 shards, leaving 1968 (656 per node) — inside the guideline.
<|call:es_delete_index|> {"index": "logs-2023.*,logs-2024.*,logs-2025.*,logs-2026.01.*,logs-2026.02.*,logs-2026.03.*"}
System Response: {"acknowledged": true} — 1020 indices deleted; active shards 14208 -> 1968.
Recovery reasoning: Confirm heap fell with the per-shard overhead gone.
<|call:es_nodes_stats|> {"metric": "jvm", "filter_path": "nodes.*.jvm.mem.heap_used_percent"}
System Response: es-d1 41%; es-d2 43%; es-d3 40%; GC pauses 8s -> 210ms; cluster-state updates 40s -> 1.2s.
Resolution: Heap pressure resolved by shard reduction alone. Root cause: unenforced retention kept 1184 daily indices x 12 shard copies = 14208 shards on 3 nodes; fixed per-shard heap overhead, invisible in cache stats, filled the old gen. ILM delete at 90 days applied; template cut from 6 primaries to 1.

Reasoning: Heap that full GC cannot reclaim means live references; disk up with flat docs means segment files are not released. Check JVM and segment stats.
<|call:es_nodes_stats|> {"metric": "jvm,indices", "filter_path": "nodes.*.jvm.mem,nodes.*.indices.segments,nodes.*.indices.store"}
System Response: heap 84% avg, old gen barely moves on full GC; segments per node 9800 (baseline ~2400); store 6.1tb, up from 4.4tb.
Recovery reasoning: 4x the normal segment count with flat docs — inspect the segments.
<|call:es_cat_segments|> {"index": "cases-*", "h": "index,segment,docs.count,docs.deleted,size"}
System Response: cases-2026.06: 214 segments, docs.deleted at 38% of docs.count, dozens of small 40-200mb segments, all searchable.
Recovery reasoning: This looks like a stalled merge scheduler and invites force-merges. But merges do not just stop: segments outlive merging only while something references them, and an open search context pins exactly that way — files on disk, per-segment structures in heap. Check open contexts first.
<|call:es_nodes_stats|> {"metric": "indices", "filter_path": "nodes.*.indices.search.open_contexts"}
System Response: open scroll contexts per node: 497, 499, 500, 498, 496, 498 — 2988 total; per-node cap search.max_open_scroll_context=500 nearly saturated.
Recovery reasoning: The exporter opens long-keepalive scrolls and dies before clearing them; at the cap new scrolls fail — the timeouts. It is idle now, so clear all.
<|call:es_clear_scroll|> {"scroll_id": "_all"}
System Response: {"succeeded": true, "num_freed": 2988}
Resolution: Heap settled at 51% after the next major GC, segments fell 9800 -> 3100 per node, store shrank as freed files deleted — no restarts. Root cause: the exporter opened 24h-keepalive scrolls and crashed before DELETE _search/scroll; leaked contexts pinned merged-away segments until the 500-per-node cap blocked new exports too. Exporter moved to search_after.

Reasoning: Lexicographic ordering (100 before 20) confirms price is text, and a field's type can never change in place — so: new index products-v4 with price as double, then an alias swap. Copy data while writes continue.
<|call:es_reindex|> {"body": {"source": {"index": "products-v3"}, "dest": {"index": "products-v4"}}, "wait_for_completion": true}
System Response: {"took": 662000, "total": 1842160, "created": 1842160, "version_conflicts": 0, "failures": []}
Recovery reasoning: Created equal to total looks finished and safe to swap — it is not: reindex works from a point-in-time snapshot taken at start, and products-v3 kept absorbing ~14 writes/sec for the 11 minutes it ran, so post-snapshot writes are missing from v4. Compare counts first.
<|call:es_count|> {"index": "products-v3,products-v4"}
System Response: products-v3: 1851400; products-v4: 1842160 — gap 9240 docs.
Recovery reasoning: 9240 matches 14/s over 11 minutes. Run a delta pass on updated_at >= the reindex start with conflicts=proceed so recopied docs overwrite.
<|call:es_reindex|> {"body": {"source": {"index": "products-v3", "query": {"range": {"updated_at": {"gte": "2026-07-03T10:14:00Z"}}}}, "dest": {"index": "products-v4"}, "conflicts": "proceed"}}
System Response: {"total": 11120, "created": 9240, "updated": 1880, "failures": []} — products-v4 count now 1851400.
Recovery reasoning: Counts converged. Swap atomically so no request sees a missing index.
<|call:es_update_aliases|> {"actions": [{"remove": {"index": "products-v3", "alias": "products"}}, {"add": {"index": "products-v4", "alias": "products", "is_write_index": true}}]}
System Response: {"acknowledged": true} — both actions in one cluster-state update; products resolves to products-v4.
Resolution: Sort on price is numeric (19.99 before 100.00), zero read downtime, counts equal at 1851400. Root cause: mappings are append-only; text-typed price required a reindex, a delta pass for the 9240 live writes, and an atomic swap.

Reasoning: Field-limit errors on a fraction of items means only docs with new field names fail. Check the write index first.
<|call:es_cat_indices|> {"index": "metrics-app-*", "h": "index,health,docs.count,store.size", "s": "index:desc"}
System Response: metrics-app-2026.07.03 green, docs.count 1204556 and rising steadily, 18.2gb.
Recovery reasoning: Green and growing suggests a marginal glitch — but that is what partial failure looks like: health reflects only the 93% of items whose field names exist; first-seen names are rejected and leave no mark. What fills the mapping?
<|call:es_get_mapping|> {"index": "metrics-app-2026.07.03", "filter_path": "*.mappings"}
System Response: 1000 mapped fields; 948 under metrics.by_container.* with names like metrics.by_container.a3f9c12de044.cpu — one field set per container ID.
Recovery reasoning: Dynamic mapping explosion: container IDs as JSON keys mint fields until the 1000-field default cap. Raising it to 5000 only delays the wall. Map by_container as flattened — one field, keys still queryable.
<|call:es_put_index_template|> {"name": "metrics-app", "body": {"index_patterns": ["metrics-app-*"], "template": {"mappings": {"properties": {"metrics": {"properties": {"by_container": {"type": "flattened"}}}}}}}}
System Response: {"acknowledged": true} — applies to new indices only.
Recovery reasoning: Templates act at index creation; roll the alias so the fix takes effect.
<|call:es_rollover|> {"alias": "metrics-app"}
System Response: {"acknowledged": true, "old_index": "metrics-app-2026.07.03", "new_index": "metrics-app-2026.07.03-000002", "rolled_over": true}
Resolution: Bulk item failures 7% -> 0 after rollover; a probe doc with a never-seen container ID indexed 201, mapping steady at 53 fields. Root cause: unbounded container IDs as object keys exploded the mapping into the 1000-field default; flattened maps the object as one field, so the limit raise is declined.

Reasoning: A node cycling out every ~20 min is failing follower checks (3 x 10s) — network, or a frozen node. Confirm from the cluster.
<|call:es_cluster_health|> {"level": "cluster"}
System Response: status yellow; data nodes 11 (es-data-4 out); relocating 28; unassigned 214; pending tasks piled up.
Recovery reasoning: Mid-storm. Read es-data-4's log around the last drop.
<|call:ssh_exec|> {"host": "es-data-4", "cmd": "tail -20 /var/log/elasticsearch/log-central.log"}
System Response: [04:31:02] INFO recovering shards ... [04:31:49] WARN master [es-master-1] failed [3] consecutive checks, disconnecting; [04:31:50] INFO joining cluster.
Recovery reasoning: The log blames the master connection — the NOC's disproved network theory. But the timestamps betray it: nothing was logged between 04:31:02 and 04:31:49. A 47s silence in the node's own log is a stop-the-world GC pause; the failed-checks line is what a frozen JVM prints after eviction. Check GC and memory.
<|call:es_nodes_stats|> {"nodes": "es-data-4", "metric": "jvm,os", "filter_path": "nodes.*.jvm,nodes.*.os"}
System Response: old-gen GC 47.3s (peers 0.4s); heap 96% before collect; os.swap.used 21.7gb; memory_lock=false; co-located logstash RSS 38gb.
Recovery reasoning: logstash's 38GB plus a 30GB unlocked heap oversubscribes the 64GB host; the kernel swapped heap pages and GC over swap takes 47s — past the 30s window. Move logstash off, disable swap, lock memory, restart.
<|call:ssh_exec|> {"host": "es-data-4", "cmd": "sudo systemctl stop logstash && sudo swapoff -a && echo 'bootstrap.memory_lock: true' | sudo tee -a /etc/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.yml && sudo systemctl restart elasticsearch"}
System Response: logstash stopped; swap off; elasticsearch restarted with mlockall true; node joined.
Resolution: es-data-4 stable through two former flap intervals, cluster green, relocating 0, GC 0.3-0.5s, p99 240ms. Root cause: a co-located logstash forced the unlocked ES heap into swap; 47s GC pauses exceeded the follower checks and each eviction churned shards. memory_lock on, co-location banned.

Reasoning: A cannot-be-upgraded refusal is version compatibility. Inspect the snapshot metadata first.
<|call:es_snapshot_get|> {"repository": "s3-archive", "snapshot": "nightly-2026-07-01"}
System Response: {"state": "SUCCESS", "version": "7.17.9", "indices": ["archive-cases"], "shards": {"total": 4, "successful": 4, "failed": 0}}
Recovery reasoning: A clean SUCCESS from 7.17.9 looks restorable into 8.11 — one major back is supported. But that field records the cluster that wrote the snapshot, not the age of what is inside: legacy-es was upgraded from 6.8, and an index keeps index.version.created forever. If archive-cases was born on 6.x, 8.x refuses it. Check the old cluster.
<|call:es_get_settings|> {"index": "archive-cases", "cluster": "http://legacy-es:9200", "filter_path": "*.settings.index.version.created"}
System Response: archive-cases: index.version.created 6082399 (6.8.23), created 2021-03-12.
Recovery reasoning: Born on 6.8.23 — no 7.x snapshot carries it into 8.x; restore is a dead end. The source is alive, so reindex-from-remote rebuilds the docs natively on 8.11, and legacy-es is already whitelisted. Create the target with the ported mapping.
<|call:es_create_index|> {"index": "archive-cases", "body": {"settings": {"number_of_shards": 4}, "mappings": {"properties": {"case_id": {"type": "keyword"}, "filed": {"type": "date"}, "body": {"type": "text"}}}}}
System Response: {"acknowledged": true, "index": "archive-cases"}
Recovery reasoning: Stream the documents from the remote in batches.
<|call:es_reindex|> {"body": {"source": {"remote": {"host": "http://legacy-es:9200"}, "index": "archive-cases", "size": 2000}, "dest": {"index": "archive-cases"}}, "wait_for_completion": false}
System Response: task 8Xa:44121 started; completed after 96m: {"total": 4120000, "created": 4120000, "failures": []}
Resolution: disco-8 count 4120000 matches legacy-es exactly and spot queries return the expected cases. Root cause: restore follows index.version.created (6.8.23), not the snapshot's writing version (7.17.9) — 8.x restores only indices created on 7.x. Reindex-from-remote bypassed snapshot lineage.

Reasoning: Parked in the warm phase usually means the allocate action cannot place shards. Read ILM's view of one stuck index.
<|call:es_ilm_explain|> {"index": "logs-2026.06.28"}
System Response: phase warm, action allocate, step check-allocation; step_info: Waiting for [8] shards to be allocated to nodes matching the given filters.
Recovery reasoning: ILM waits on allocation filters. Check the warm nodes exist and have room.
<|call:es_cat_nodes|> {"h": "name,node.role,disk.used_percent,heap.percent", "v": true}
System Response: es-warm-1..4 role=d disk 22-26% heap 31-38; es-hot-1..4 role=d disk 85-88; all 8 data nodes present.
Recovery reasoning: Four healthy warm nodes with 75% free disk make the tier look ready and suggest ILM is wedged — but cat/nodes shows roles, never the custom attributes the allocate filter matches on; the filter can be unsatisfiable while every node looks fine. Allocation explain prints each node's attributes.
<|call:es_allocation_explain|> {"index": "logs-2026.06.28", "shard": 0, "primary": true}
System Response: can_remain: no (require box_type=warm violated); can_move_to_other_node: no — es-warm-1..4: node_attributes {"temp": "warm"} lack [box_type]; es-hot-1..4: box_type=hot.
Recovery reasoning: Provisioning tags warm nodes node.attr.temp=warm while ILM requires box_type=warm — nothing satisfies the filter, so ILM waits forever. Node attributes are static, index settings dynamic: re-key the requirement on the stuck indices now and fix the policy for future ones.
<|call:es_put_settings|> {"index": "logs-2026.06.*", "body": {"index.routing.allocation.require.box_type": null, "index.routing.allocation.require.temp": "warm"}}
System Response: {"acknowledged": true} — applied to 22 indices; reroute triggered.
Resolution: 64 of 176 shards began relocating (rest queued behind recovery throttles), hot disk 87% -> 81% and projected under 70% by morning. Root cause: attribute-key mismatch — warm nodes carried temp=warm while ILM required box_type=warm, an unsatisfiable filter stranding 22 indices (176 shards) on hot. Provisioning now emits box_type too.

Reasoning: Heap that jumped with an aggregation release points at what the agg loads. Verify the obvious consumers first: caches and hit rates.
<|call:es_nodes_stats|> {"metric": "indices,jvm", "filter_path": "nodes.*.indices.query_cache,nodes.*.indices.request_cache,nodes.*.jvm.mem"}
System Response: query_cache 610mb hit 94%; request_cache 180mb hit 88%; heap 26.1gb of 30gb per node (old gen 87%).
Recovery reasoning: Strong hit ratios read as "memory well spent, look elsewhere" — but the caches explain under 1GB of a 26GB residency; the story is in what those stats did not itemize. The classic resident after an aggregation release is fielddata — measure it.
<|call:es_cat_fielddata|> {"h": "node,field,size", "v": true}
System Response: es-c1 author 9.8gb; es-c2 author 9.8gb; es-c3 author 9.7gb — nothing else above 40mb.
Recovery reasoning: 29.3GB on author means aggregating on a text field — fielddata on text is off by default because it uninverts the field into heap, so someone enabled it. Check the mapping for a keyword field.
<|call:es_get_mapping|> {"index": "articles", "filter_path": "*.mappings.properties.author"}
System Response: author: {"type": "text", "fielddata": true, "fields": {"raw": {"type": "keyword"}}}
Recovery reasoning: author.raw already exists — aggs belong there, on doc_values, not heap. fielddata is one of the few text parameters legally updatable in place, so flip it off; the widget query moves to author.raw.
<|call:es_put_mapping|> {"index": "articles", "body": {"properties": {"author": {"type": "text", "fielddata": false}}}}
System Response: {"acknowledged": true}
Recovery reasoning: Structures already built stay resident until evicted — clear the fielddata cache now.
<|call:es_clear_cache|> {"index": "articles", "fielddata": true}
System Response: {"_shards": {"total": 12, "successful": 12}} — 29.3gb released; old gen 87% -> 52% after next GC.
Resolution: p99 back to 180ms (doc_values aggs need no heap-resident uninverted index); heap alerts cleared. Root cause: the widget enabled fielddata on text field author instead of using the existing author.raw keyword subfield, uninverting ~9.8GB into each node's heap.

Reasoning: An index 16x past its ceiling means ILM stopped executing, not a wrong threshold. Confirm the policy anyway.
<|call:es_get_ilm_policy|> {"policy": "logs-policy"}
System Response: hot.rollover: max_primary_shard_size 50gb, max_age 1d; delete: min_age 30d; version 4, modified 2026-05-11.
Recovery reasoning: The policy is right and untouched since May — which proves nothing: a policy is the plan, not the execution. ILM keeps per-index state and can sit failing a step indefinitely. Read the newest index's ILM state.
<|call:es_ilm_explain|> {"index": "logs-app-000017"}
System Response: phase hot, action rollover, step ERROR, failed_step check-rollover-ready; step_info: index [logs-app-000017] is not the write index for alias [logs-app]; failed_step_retry_count 1240.
Recovery reasoning: ILM refuses to roll an index that is not the alias's write index. Something rewired the alias — inspect its flags.
<|call:es_get_alias|> {"index": "logs-app-*", "name": "logs-app"}
System Response: logs-app-000016: is_write_index true; logs-app-000017: is_write_index false.
Recovery reasoning: A manual alias rebuild nine days ago set is_write_index back on the already-rolled 000016 — writes poured into it since (ILM sees it as rolled, so it grows unbounded) while ILM errors on 000017, which never receives a byte. Flip the flags atomically; ILM auto-retries once the alias is sane.
<|call:es_update_aliases|> {"actions": [{"add": {"index": "logs-app-000016", "alias": "logs-app", "is_write_index": false}}, {"add": {"index": "logs-app-000017", "alias": "logs-app", "is_write_index": true}}]}
System Response: {"acknowledged": true} — both flag changes in one cluster-state update; writes now route to logs-app-000017.
Resolution: On the next auto-retry check-rollover-ready cleared; 000017 ingests and will roll at 50GB while 000016 stops growing and ages toward its 30d delete. Root cause: an alias rebuild set is_write_index on the already-rolled 000016; ILM never rolls a non-write index and had failed check-rollover-ready 1240 times while it absorbed nine days of writes.

Reasoning: Flapping between full and empty per request smells like the LB spraying traffic across two realities. Check health through the LB.
<|call:es_cluster_health|> {"endpoint": "https://lb-es.prod:9200"}
System Response: cluster_name search-prod, status green, number_of_nodes 3, active_shards_percent 100.0.
Recovery reasoning: Green with 100% active looks fine — but the node count is the tell: four hosts sit behind this LB, health says three. This call hit a 3-node cluster, so the 0-hit answers come from something on 9200 that passes the LB check. Ask es-prod-4.
<|call:es_cat_nodes|> {"endpoint": "https://es-prod-4:9200", "h": "name,node.role,master", "v": true}
